        <h2>How Does Targeted KB Compression Work?</h2>
        <p>
          When compressing an image or document down to a specific threshold (e.g., exactly 20KB, 50KB, or 100KB), a standard linear compression algorithm is inadequate because different images contain varying levels of visual entropy. An image with broad flat color areas (such as a clear background portrait) compresses far more efficiently than an image with complex, high-frequency details (such as a scanned document containing fine handwritten text).
        </p>

        <h3>1. Lossy vs. Lossless Quantization</h3>
        <p>
          For raster formats like <strong>JPEG</strong> and <strong>WebP</strong>, ConvertKB controls the quantization tables. The browser calculates discrete cosine transform coefficients in 8x8 pixel blocks. By gently removing high-frequency details that the human eye cannot discern at standard viewing distances, the file size drops dramatically while sharpness around key contours (such as facial borders or signature pen strokes) remains crisp and legible.
        </p>

        <h3>2. PNG Alpha Layer Optimization</h3>
        <p>
          PNG is inherently lossless, which makes it an ideal format for official seals and digital signatures requiring transparency. However, unoptimized PNGs can be unnecessarily heavy. ConvertKB systematically palettizes 24-bit PNGs down to adaptive 8-bit color arrays (indexed PNG-8), discarding redundant color palettes while preserving edge sharpness and transparent backgrounds.
        </p>

        <h3>3. PDF Document Structure Compression</h3>
        <p>
          A PDF file is essentially an envelope containing embedded text objects, vector instructions, font glyphs, and raster image scans. When users compress a PDF to 100KB, 200KB, or 500KB on ConvertKB:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Metadata Stripping:</strong> Unused XML metadata tags, thumbnail caches, color profiles, and software markers are removed.</li>
          <li><strong>Raster Downsampling:</strong> Scanned receipts and document pages embedded at 300+ DPI are downsampled to a clean 150 DPI—the standard reading resolution for screen reviews.</li>
          <li><strong>Font Subsetting:</strong> Embedded fonts are flattened or subsetted so only characters actually used within the document remain stored in the file envelope.</li>
        </ul>

